findings.description:
"Port all BulkForce implementations to `@ti.kernel`" is one line. The tick as built includes:

- per-agent profile indices and per-agent `k` caps;
- `active_indices` subset evaluation;
- predator-prey cross-population forces;
- repulsors (slice 314);
- world-bounds wrap policy (slice 313);
- terrain-aware movement, which needs environment-layer grids on the device;
- spawn and despawn through `EntityManager` (slice 302).

None is mentioned. Taichi-lineage fields are statically sized, so a changing N needs a capacity-plus-alive-mask design, and something must own the host-to-device sync when the server spawns entities. This should be confirmed against the Quadrants docs.

Threading is also unaddressed. Slice 316 runs `sim.step` on a dedicated worker thread. The document does not say where the Quadrants runtime is initialized or whether kernels may be called from a non-main thread. Numba's first-call JIT pause will show up as a multi-second tick overrun unless compiled ahead of time or warmed up.
